\begin_layout Title
Does the NYPD's Stop, Question and Frisk Program Deter Crime?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 26, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The New York City stop, question and frisk program is a practice of the
 New York City Police Department by which police officers are able to stop
 and question pedestrians, as well as frisk them for weapons and other contraban
d.
 Proponents of the measure claim that it is aimed at reducing crime by stopping,
 questioning and searching individuals suspectd of wrongdoing.
 The police Commissioner has claimed that stop-and-frisk has saved lived
 and driven down crime.
 However, many minority advocacy groups oppose the law because they believe
 it is a form of racial profiling.
 In fact, several officers have alleged in testimony that many of their
 colleagues target young black men and other minorities.
 Also, in 2012, almost 90 percent of stop-and-frisk incidents citywide yielded
 no drugs, guns, open containers, or anything else illegal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to answer this question, I intend to employ a difference-in-difference
 strategy.
 Since New York City is the only treatment group, and to my knowledge, there
 is no comparable control group, then in order to obtain reasonable estimates
 and perform inference, I will apply the Sythetic Control Approach.
 I will construct a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

sythetic New York City
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as a control by using a weighted average of other cities in the region;
 I will endogenously select the weights so that the synthetic control matches
 up well with New York City in the pre-treatment period.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This method overcomes the identification problem because there are no systematic
 differences between the treatment and control groups.
 Also, no other time-varying factor should affect my estimates because I
 will not be identifying off of time-series variation.
 Most importantly, my identifying assumption is that absent the treatment,
 the change in the outcome for the treatment and control groups would have
 been the same over time.
 I expect this assumption to hold because I will construct my control group
 in such a way that it matches with the treatment group in the pre-treatment
 period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, this project is feasible because the data is readily available.
 Every time a police officer stops a person in NYC, the officer is supposed
 to fill out a form to record the details of the stop.
 So, both the New York Police Department and the New York Civil Liberties
 Union provide annual and quarterly data on the stop-and-frisk program.
 The electroic database includes nearly all of the data recorded by the
 police officer after a stop.
 In addition, I would be able to obtain annual and quarterly crime data
 from the NYPD.
\end_layout

